course: dictionary
lesson: 13656
title: Inflection: changing word endings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An inflection is a change to a word, usually its ending, that shows a different meaning in grammar. «Walk» becomes «walks» when one person walks, «walked» when it happened yesterday, and «walking» when it is happening now.
explain: The most common endings are «-s», «-ed», «-ing», «-er» and «-est». «The tall tree» becomes «the taller tree» and then «the tallest tree». The base word stays the same, and the ending does the work.
explain: Your voice can have inflection too. When you say «You're coming too?» your voice rises at the end, and that rise tells the listener it is a question. The same words said with a falling voice sound like a statement.
fact: An inflection is a change in the form of a word, usually at its end.
fact: Inflections can show tense (when something happens), number (one or more) and other grammar details.
fact: «Walk», «walks», «walked» and «walking» are all forms of the same word.
fact: In speaking, inflection means the rise and fall of your voice, which shows questions and feelings.
fact: In maths, a point of inflection is where a curve changes the way it bends.
exercise: In «The bird sings», which word has the inflection «-s»? | bird | sweet | the | *sings
exercise: «She jumped over the puddle.» What ending shows that the jumping happened in the past? | -est | -ing | *-ed | -s
exercise: When you ask «You're coming too?», what does your rising voice show? | That you are asleep | That it is a shout | That it is a joke only | *That it is a question
exercise: Which word is the «-ing» form of «swim»? | swims | swimmed | *swimming | swam
exercise: In maths, what is a point of inflection? | The highest point on a hill | Where a line meets the edge of the page | *Where a curve changes the way it bends | The number zero
test: What ending turns «tall» into the word for the most tall? | -er | *-est | -ing | -ed
test: «The dog barks.» Which word shows only one dog is barking? | *barks | the | barking | dog
exam: Which word is the plural of «cat», meaning more than one cat? | cating | catted | *cats | catest
